<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Lingones</h1><p class="st-meta">400 BCE – 51 BCE (approx.) · capital Andematunnum</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Gallic people of the Langres plateau around the sources of the Seine and Marne, who stayed loyal to Rome during the great revolt of 52 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Lingones held the uplands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langres">Andematunnum</a> (Langres), between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sequani">Sequani</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/aedui">Aedui</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/leuci">Leuci</a>, astride the watershed between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Saône</a> basins. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> record that part of the people joined the Celtic migrations into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> around 400 BCE and settled near the Adriatic coast south of the Po.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Like their neighbours they lived in fortified oppida, struck their own coins and profited from the routes linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Saône</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a>. Little is known of their institutions, but they were large enough to supply <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Caesar</a>'s armies with grain.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Lingones refused to join <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a> in 52 BCE and were rewarded with the status of an allied civitas under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. In 70 CE their nobleman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-sabinus">Julius Sabinus</a> joined the revolt of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/batavi">Batavi</a> and proclaimed himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Caesar</a>; after its failure his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epponina">Epponina</a> hid him for nine years before both were executed.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lingones.jpg" alt="Lingones" width="320" height="325"><figcaption>Image: Feitscherg · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_Gallia_Tribes_Towns.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>400 BCE – 51 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Andematunnum</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gaulish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-226&amp;focus=lingones" aria-label="Lingones on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4804 501 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lingones lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5124 757L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5124 757h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5124 757h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Andematunnum. <a href="/atlas?year=-226&amp;focus=lingones">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

